//! The CONTROL ARM for `agent_seam.awl`: the same workflow, the same action
//! shape, the same call — with the `agent` marker absent.
//!
//! `agent` is a DECLARATION classification. It tells a worker to hand the call
//! to an agent harness instead of a typed handler, and it must change nothing
//! about how the flow lowers. This document exists so that claim is CHECKED
//! rather than asserted: `agent_marked_and_unmarked_lower_identically` diffs
//! the two committed MIR goldens and fails if they ever differ.
//!
//! Keep this file in lockstep with `agent_seam.awl`. If the two drift for any
//! reason other than the marker, the comparison stops being a control and
//! quietly becomes two unrelated fixtures that happen to both compile.
//!
//! The WORKFLOW NAME must differ: a deployed package's entry module is named
//! from it, and two workflows sharing one name cannot be loaded together (the
//! BC-4 differential loads the whole covered corpus at once and refuses the
//! duplicate outright). The name is therefore the one thing the golden
//! comparison normalises, and it normalises nothing else.
workflow ask_the_agent_control
  input question: String
  outcome answered: type Answer, route success

type Answer { reply: String }

/// The canonical agent-outcome record, declared by the author under a name of
/// their own choosing: the checker demands the STRUCTURE, not a blessed name.
type Reply { text: String, stop_reason: String }

worker assistants
  action ask(prompt: String) -> Reply
    timeout 10m

step ask
  ask(prompt: question) -> reply
  route answered(reply: reply.text)
